    id: 'fa-valuation-methods', title: 'Valuation Purpose & Relative Methods', subtitle: 'Business value, market capitalization, revenue and earnings multiples, and method limitations.',
    summary: `# Valuation Purpose & Relative Methods\n\n## What business valuation estimates\nBusiness valuation estimates the economic value of a whole company or business unit for decisions such as investment, financing, acquisition or sale. Value depends on expected future benefits, risk and the claim being valued. Equity value belongs to shareholders; enterprise value represents operating value available to all capital providers before debt and other non-equity claims are deducted.\n\n### Market capitalization\nFor a publicly traded company, market capitalization is share price multiplied by shares outstanding. It is an observable market value of equity at a point in time, not enterprise value. It changes with market price and does not by itself account for debt or excess cash.\n\n### Revenue and earnings multiples\nThe times-revenue method applies a revenue multiple to a revenue base. The earnings-multiplier approach applies a multiple to earnings, often providing more information about profitability than revenue alone. Multiples should be selected from comparable firms and periods. They are relative methods: a market multiple reflects expectations, risk, growth, accounting and market conditions.\n\n### Choosing and communicating a method\nRelative valuation can be useful when comparable data are available, but a multiple is not an absolute answer. The lecture presents DCF as a method based on projected future cash flows and a discount rate. Use the method that matches the question, define whether the result is equity or enterprise value, disclose the inputs and test limitations.`,
