    summary: `# Time Value, WACC & Discounting\n\n## Future cash flows in today’s terms\nMoney available sooner can be invested and is generally worth more than the same amount received later. Discounting converts a future cash flow to present value: $PV_t = CF_t/(1+r)^t$, where $r$ reflects time and risk and $t$ is the number of periods. Present values can then be added when cash flows are defined consistently.\n\n### Weighted average cost of capital\nThe WACC is the weighted average required return from equity and debt capital. In the course example, $WACC = (E/V)R_e + (D/V)R_d(1-T)$, where equity and debt weights sum to one, $R_e$ is the cost of equity, $R_d$ the cost of debt, and $T$ the tax rate. The after-tax debt term reflects the tax effect of interest in the model. The discount rate must match the cash flow’s risk and financing definition.\n\n### NPV and IRR\nThe present value of projected cash flows is closely related to NPV. In project analysis, NPV subtracts the initial investment from the discounted future cash flows. IRR is the discount rate that makes NPV equal zero. The lecture presents IRR as an additional indicator, not a replacement for understanding cash-flow scale and assumptions.\n\n### Consistency checks\nUse end-of-year timing consistently unless the model specifies otherwise. Match unlevered cash flows with WACC and equity cash flows with the required return on equity. WACC is not simply the interest rate on the latest loan: it includes both debt and equity capital costs and the capital structure.`,
