    summary: `# DCF Forecasts & Terminal Value\n\n## The two-stage DCF\nA discounted cash-flow valuation estimates value from future unlevered free cash flows. The explicit stage often forecasts about five years, supported by linked income statement, balance sheet and cash-flow assumptions. A continuing business is not assumed to disappear at the end of that horizon; cash flows after the forecast period are summarized by terminal value.\n\n### Forecasting unlevered free cash flow\nThe supplied slides build free cash flow from operating cash flow less investment in fixed assets and exclude financing flows. In a more formal unlevered formulation, operating cash generated after tax and required reinvestment is available to both debt and equity providers. Keep this definition consistent with the model and its WACC.\n\n### Terminal value methods\nThe perpetuity-growth method assumes mature cash flow grows at a stable long-term rate $g$ and uses $TV_n = FCF_{n+1}/(WACC-g)$, with $g < WACC$. The exit-multiple method applies a selected market multiple, such as EV/EBITDA, to a terminal operating metric. The lecture notes that exit multiples incorporate current market valuation into DCF and can dominate total value, making assumptions especially important.\n\n### Present value and sensitivity\nDiscount forecast-year cash flows and terminal value back to the valuation date. Terminal value is treated as a cash flow at the end of the explicit horizon. Small changes in WACC, long-term growth, revenue growth or margins can materially alter valuation. Test reasonable scenarios and explain that the estimate depends on assumptions rather than presenting it as certain.`,
