    summary: `# Enterprise-to-Equity Value & Valuation Challenge\n\n## From operating value to shareholder value\nA DCF of unlevered free cash flow discounted at WACC produces enterprise value, the value of operations available to all capital providers. To reach equity value, add non-operating assets not included in operating cash flow, such as excess cash, marketable securities and equity investments. Then subtract debt and other non-equity claims, including leases, preferred stock and non-controlling interests.\n\n### Equity value per share\nAfter deriving total equity value, divide by shares outstanding to estimate value per share. Keep units consistent and avoid subtracting financing claims twice. Cash must be identified as operating or excess/non-operating consistently; debt must be included in the bridge if the DCF cash flows are unlevered.\n\n### Integrated challenge workflow\nThe valuation challenge in the lecture builds balance sheet, profit and loss and cash-flow projections for 2025–2030, calculates free cash flow, estimates WACC and terminal value, discounts cash flows, derives enterprise value, adds cash, subtracts loans and derives equity value. This sequence relies on internally consistent statements and assumptions.\n\n### Communicating value\nReport the forecast horizon, growth and margin assumptions, tax and reinvestment assumptions, WACC components, terminal method and bridge from enterprise to equity value. Explain the largest sensitivities and use scenarios where small assumption changes create material differences. A valuation is a reasoned estimate, not a guaranteed transaction price.`,
