const expandedGuides = {
  'fa-accounting-foundations': `## From economic event to standardized account\n\nThe lectures place accounting inside Portugal’s standardization framework. Standardized accounting rules provide a common structure for recording and comparing organizations across time and between firms. The materials refer to the Portuguese accounting normalization system and its historical chart-of-accounts framework, the POC; the practical lesson is that a company uses an organized chart of accounts rather than inventing labels each time. The account has a title identifying the kind of resource, claim, income or cost, and extensions that record individual movements.\n\nThe basic qualitative aims are that information should be relevant to a decision, reliable enough to trust, understandable to its intended users and comparable across periods or firms. Historical cost records an acquired asset using its purchase or production cost, while accrual accounting places transactions in the period in which the economic activity occurs. These principles work together: standardization makes entries comparable, and period attribution makes them meaningful.\n\n### Worked transaction sequence\n\nSuppose a firm buys €1,000 of inventory, paying €500 now and owing €500. The entry is debit Inventory €1,000, credit Cash €500 and credit Accounts Payable €500. When it later sells the goods for €1,500 cash, record debit Cash €1,500 and credit Revenue €1,500. Separately recognize debit COGS €1,000 and credit Inventory €1,000. The gross profit is €500. The sale creates revenue and a cost; neither entry is the same thing as the cash receipt alone.\n\nThis is the practical test for every entry: identify what happened, select the accounts affected, determine which balances increased or decreased, then make total debits equal total credits. A balanced entry is necessary, but the classification and period must also be correct.`,
  'fa-period-end-adjustments': `## Inventory, estimates and the close\n\nInventory cost-flow assumptions change the allocation between COGS and ending inventory, even when the physical units sold are unchanged. The class keyboard example has 1,000 keyboards available: 250 at €6.50, 600 at €5.70 and 150 at €5.00. The weighted average unit cost is $(250×6.50 + 600×5.70 + 150×5.00)/1,000 = €5.80$. FIFO assigns the oldest acquisition costs to goods sold; LIFO assigns the latest costs to goods sold in the comparison shown in the lecture. The resulting COGS and gross margins differ. The analyst must state the convention and not compare figures calculated under different assumptions as if they were identical.\n\n### Period-end adjustments step by step\n\nFor straight-line depreciation, subtract any residual value from cost and divide by useful life. A €12,000 asset with zero residual value and a four-year life generates €3,000 depreciation each year. Debit Depreciation Expense and credit Accumulated Depreciation. The expense lowers profit and accumulated depreciation lowers the asset’s net carrying amount; neither side represents a new cash payment in the depreciation year. A provision is an estimate of an obligation or expected loss, such as a doubtful receivable. It is not money placed in a separate bank account.\n\nAccruals record activity already earned or incurred but not yet settled in cash. Deferrals postpone revenue or expense recognition when cash or billing precedes delivery or consumption. The Byte Bakery case asks students to adjust depreciation, insurance used, accrued salaries and deferred revenue before calculating tax. The sequence matters: make all accounting adjustments first, compute adjusted earnings before tax, and only then apply the case’s tax rate. Finish by checking the balance sheet equation and whether the adjustments explain cash movement.`,
  'fa-balance-income-statements': `## Reading the statements as one model\n\nThe lecture distinguishes a balance sheet at a date from an income statement over a period. On the balance sheet, current assets include cash, receivables and inventory; longer-lived resources include equipment and buildings. Liabilities include supplier balances, accruals and loans. Equity is the residual claim: assets less liabilities. The income statement begins with sales, subtracts COGS to obtain gross profit, then operating expenses to reach EBIT, interest to reach EBT, and tax to reach net income. Keep the subtotals separate because each answers a different question about the business.\n\n### DataVision worked close\n\nThe source case begins with provisional Year 2 sales of €500,000, COGS of €300,000, salaries of €90,000, other operating costs of €48,000 and interest of €8,000. The close adds €20,000 depreciation, €5,000 accrued bonuses, reverses €15,000 of revenue for work not delivered until Year 3, and records €4,000 expected bad debt. Final sales are €485,000. Gross profit is €185,000. EBIT is €18,000 after salaries of €95,000, other costs of €48,000, depreciation of €20,000 and bad-debt expense of €4,000. EBT is €10,000 after interest; tax at 21% is €2,100; net income is €7,900.\n\nThe closing balance sheet records accrued salaries €5,000, deferred revenue €15,000 and tax payable €2,100. The source case gives total assets of €356,000 and uses equity as the residual after known liabilities because the information is incomplete. That residual is a balancing identity, not evidence that the equity estimate is independently verified. When evidence is missing, label the limitation instead of inventing a figure.`,
  'fa-cash-flow-integration': `## A full profit-to-cash reconciliation\n\nThe challenge slides show why the three statements must agree. Company ABC’s 2025 close reports net loss of €219,165, depreciation of €66,665, provisions of €80,000, receivables of €830,000, inventory of €930,000, payables of €870,000, other liabilities of €50,000, fixed equipment of €445,000 and financing raised through €1,050,000 loans plus €500,000 share capital. If opening operating balances are zero, operating cash flow by the indirect bridge is approximately $-219,165 + 66,665 + 80,000 - 830,000 - 930,000 + 870,000 + 50,000 = -€912,500$. The net fixed-asset investment is €445,000, so investing cash flow is −€445,000. Financing cash flow is €1,550,000. Net cash movement is €192,500, matching the reported closing cash.\n\nThis reconciliation makes several signs intuitive. Depreciation and the non-cash provision are added back to profit. Growth in receivables and inventory absorbs cash. Growth in payables and other liabilities temporarily supplies cash. Buying equipment is an investing outflow. Borrowing and issuing shares are financing inflows, not revenue.\n\n### Cash-flow checks\n\nThe closing cash in the statement of cash flows must match cash on the balance sheet. Free cash flow in the course examples is operating cash flow less fixed-asset investment. For a valuation, remove financing flows from the operating cash-flow stream before discounting unlevered cash flow at WACC. If the bridge fails, revisit opening balances, working-capital movements, non-cash entries and investment amounts instead of inserting an unexplained plug.`,
  'fa-common-size-trends': `## Trend and peer analysis in practice\n\nVertical analysis divides each income-statement item by sales and each balance-sheet item by total assets. Horizontal analysis compares both the amount and percentage change between periods. A common-size percentage can move because its numerator changed, because its denominator changed faster, or both. For example, receivables can rise as a share of assets because credit sales grew, because collection slowed, or because other assets shrank. The percentage identifies a question; it does not settle the cause.\n\nThe lecture recommends three to five years for identifying trends where available. Two periods show movement but may not distinguish a trend from normal fluctuation. Quarterly figures require seasonal comparisons: compare the same quarter across years when sales peak in a particular season. A trend can also change suddenly, so extrapolation is not a forecast guarantee.\n\n### Joe’s Gadgets common-size example\n\nIn Joe’s Gadgets, COGS rises from 40% of sales in 2016 to 55% in 2017, while S&A falls from 20% to 17.5%. EBIT margin declines from 26.7% to 15%; net margin declines from 14% to about 5.25%. On the balance sheet, receivables and inventory each rise from 11.8% to 18.1% of assets. These figures point to cost pressure and working-capital questions. The peer comparison shows whether the issue may be industry-wide, but comparability can be weak for conglomerates, dominant firms or companies with different accounting choices and business mixes. State which observation is evidence and which explanation remains a hypothesis.`,
  'fa-liquidity-activity-ratios': `## Formula conventions and worked activity ratios\n\nFor this class, use ending balance-sheet values unless the case explicitly supplies an average-balance method. The current ratio is $Current\ Assets/Current\ Liabilities$. The quick ratio is $(Current\ Assets-Inventory)/Current\ Liabilities$. Inventory turnover is $COGS/Ending\ Inventory$. Days sales outstanding is $Accounts\ Receivable/(Sales/365)$, equivalently receivables divided by sales and multiplied by 365. Fixed-asset turnover is $Sales/Net\ Fixed\ Assets$; total-asset turnover is $Sales/Total\ Assets$. Always label turnover as “times” and DSO as days.\n\n### Joe’s Gadgets: collection and efficiency\n\nFor 2016, Joe’s Gadgets has current assets €7.0m, inventory €2.0m and current liabilities €4.5m. Current ratio is 1.56 and quick ratio 1.11. With COGS €6m, inventory turnover is 3.0 times. Receivables of €2m and sales of €15m give DSO $2/(15/365)=48.67$ days. Fixed assets of €10m give fixed-asset turnover 1.50; total assets of €17m give total-asset turnover 0.88.\n\nIn 2017, current and quick ratios are 1.58 and 1.01; inventory turnover falls to 2.75 times; DSO rises to 73 days; fixed-asset and total-asset turnover are 1.82 and 0.91. The combination suggests slower collections and weaker inventory movement even though fixed assets generate more sales. Seasonality can distort a year-end DSO: compare the balance-sheet date with the sales pattern and collection terms before blaming credit management. Higher turnover is not automatically better if it creates stockouts or lost sales.`,
  'fa-debt-profitability-ratios': `## Formula map and leverage interpretation\n\nUse $Total\ Debt/Total\ Assets$ for debt-to-assets and $Total\ Debt/Equity$ for debt-to-equity. The first is generally below 1 because debt is one component of total assets’ financing; the second can exceed 1 when debt is greater than equity. Times interest earned is $EBIT/Interest\ Expense$. Gross profit margin is $(Sales-COGS)/Sales$; net profit margin is $Net\ Income/Sales$; ROA is $Net\ Income/Total\ Assets$; and ROE is $Net\ Income/Equity$. Keep the earnings measure and balance-sheet denominator consistent when comparing firms.\n\nJoe’s Gadgets illustrates the combined story. Debt-to-assets rises from 58.8% in 2016 to 68.0% in 2017, debt-to-equity from 142.9% to 212.8%, and interest coverage falls from 4.0 to 2.0 times. Gross margin falls from 60% to 45%, net margin from 14% to 5.25%, ROA from 12.4% to 4.76%, and ROE from 30.0% to 14.9%. The profitability decline is paired with higher leverage and less interest cushion.\n\nA high ROE is not automatically a sign of stronger operations. Debt can shrink the equity denominator and amplify returns in good periods, while fixed interest claims make a downturn more dangerous. Compare ROA and ROE, inspect operating margins, test interest coverage, and ask whether the cash-flow pattern can service debt. The lecture’s preference for TIE above 2 is a broad rule of thumb, not a universal covenant or proof of safety.`,
  'fa-market-ratios-diagnosis': `## Market measures and the Joe’s Gadgets diagnosis\n\nThe class formulas are $EPS=Net\ Income/Shares$, $P/E=Share\ Price/EPS$, $Market-to-Book=Share\ Price/Book\ Value\ per\ Share$, and $Dividend\ Yield=Dividend\ per\ Share/Share\ Price$. In Joe’s Gadgets, EPS falls from €1.05 to €0.525, while price falls from €25 to €17.50. P/E therefore rises from 23.81 to 33.33 because earnings fell proportionally faster than price. Market-to-book falls from 7.14 to 4.96 as book value per share and market price move differently; dividend yield rises from 2.0% to about 2.86% because the €0.50 dividend is divided by a lower share price.\n\nAgainst 2017 industry averages, Joe’s Gadgets has adequate current liquidity, but weaker inventory turnover and a longer DSO. Fixed-asset turnover is stronger. Debt ratios are riskier, interest coverage is lower, and margins and returns have declined. COGS is high versus the prior year but similar interpretation requires industry context. The dividend yield looks higher, yet debt pressure and falling earnings mean the dividend may not be sustainable.\n\nA high P/E can reflect growth expectations, but in this case it can also rise because earnings have collapsed. A high market-to-book can reflect unrecorded brands or growth; a low ratio may reflect risk or weak prospects. P/E is not meaningful with negative earnings. Dividend yield is not a guaranteed return. Explain the direction of the denominator before calling a market ratio cheap or expensive.`,
  'fa-valuation-methods': `## Book value, liquidation value and relative methods\n\nThe course distinguishes several valuation bases. Book value is assets less liabilities as shown on the balance sheet; it is an accounting residual, not necessarily the price a buyer would pay. Liquidation value estimates net cash from selling assets and paying liabilities now. Market capitalization is share price multiplied by shares outstanding and values public equity. A recent financing round can provide a private-company market signal, but it is tied to that round’s terms and date.\n\nThe times-revenue method applies an industry- and environment-dependent revenue multiple. An earnings multiplier uses a profit measure and is more sensitive to margins and earnings quality. In the lecture example, a €50 share price divided by €5 EPS gives 10 times earnings; another firm at €65 with the same €5 EPS trades at 13 times. That is a relative comparison, not proof that either company has an absolute fair value. DCF instead forecasts cash flows and discounts for time and risk.\n\n### Match the method to the question\n\nMarket capitalization is equity value. An enterprise value bridge starts from operating value, then considers cash and debt claims. Do not compare EV-based multiples with equity-based multiples without reconciling the numerator. Use multiples from genuinely comparable businesses, define the earnings or revenue period, and explain growth, risk and accounting differences. When reliable forecasts are available, DCF can make assumptions explicit; when they are not, a precise DCF can create false confidence rather than accuracy.`,
  'fa-wacc-discounting': `## WACC from the Startup Z case\n\nThe Startup Z example finances 65% of capital with debt costing 7.2% and 35% with equity costing 12%; the tax rate is 21%. The after-tax debt cost is $7.2\%×(1-0.21)=5.688\%$. WACC is $0.35×12\%+0.65×5.688\%=8.0\%$ after rounding. This is not simply the loan rate: it weights the cost of both financing sources and reflects the debt tax effect used in the course model.\n\n### Discounting, NPV and IRR\n\nA cash flow $CF_t$ received at year end has present value $CF_t/(1+r)^t$. At 8%, €10,000 received in year 2 is worth €10,000/1.08² = about €8,573 today. Add the present values of the modeled future cash flows to get the DCF value. For a project, NPV then subtracts the initial investment. Positive NPV means the discounted inflows exceed that initial cost at the chosen required return; it does not guarantee that the forecast will occur. IRR is the rate that makes NPV equal zero.\n\nUse WACC for unlevered operating cash flows. The rate must match the period, currency, inflation basis and risk of the cash flow. A higher discount rate lowers present value; a later cash flow is discounted for more periods. The course’s project example has a positive NPV of about $2.307m$ when discounted cash flows total about $13.307m$ against an €11m initial cost.`,
  'fa-dcf-terminal-value': `## Terminal value with a complete worked example\n\nThe lecture’s five-year exercise forecasts FCF of €10m, €12m, €15m, €18m and €20m, uses WACC of 10%, and assumes long-run growth of 3%. Discount the explicit flows: approximately €9.09m, €9.92m, €11.27m, €12.29m and €12.42m, totalling about €55.0m. Year 6 FCF is €20m×1.03=€20.6m. The perpetuity-growth terminal value at year 5 is $20.6/(0.10-0.03)=€294.3m$. Discount it five years: €294.3m/1.10⁵≈€182.7m. Enterprise value is approximately €55.0m+€182.7m=€237.7m.\n\nThe exit-multiple approach instead applies a market multiple, often EV/EBITDA, to a terminal-year measure. It is intuitive when considering a future sale but imports current market pricing into a DCF. Both methods are sensitive to assumptions; terminal value often forms a large share of total value.\n\nIn the Startup Z example, the course shows a WACC of 8% and forecast cash flows of −€58,000, −€18,190, €21,980, €41,980 and €41,980, producing an NPV around €7,577 under the stated assumptions. A different maintenance-investment assumption changes later FCF and valuation. This is why a forecast must specify whether investment merely expands capacity or is required to sustain existing operations.`,
  'fa-equity-value-challenge': `## The six-step bridge in Company X\n\nFollow the source lecture’s unlevered sequence: forecast unlevered free cash flow; calculate terminal value; discount forecast flows and terminal value at WACC; add non-operating assets; subtract debt and other non-equity claims; then divide equity value by shares outstanding. In the 2024 Company X example, the discounted DCF gives enterprise value of €158.719k. Add cash of €45.473k and subtract loans of €80k: equity value is €124.192k. If there were 10,000 shares, value per share would be €12.4192. Do not include payables as debt automatically without following the model’s defined operating-liability treatment; do not subtract any claim twice.\n\n### Connect operating forecasts to the bridge\n\nThe Company X model starts from five years of income statements, balance sheets and cash flows. The cash-flow statement supplies operating and investment flows; financing informs capital structure and WACC. Its listed assumptions include average growth of 4%, WACC 10%, equity weight 38%, debt weight 62%, tax 19%, return on equity 18% and interest rate 5%. The slides label the bridge components explicitly: enterprise value first, then cash added and loans subtracted to reach equity value.\n\nA defensible presentation states the forecast horizon, growth and margin assumptions, WACC inputs, terminal method and value bridge. Reconcile projected statements before valuation. Show how the result changes with WACC and terminal assumptions, and distinguish a model estimate from a transaction price.`,
};

const segments = [...chapterOne, ...chapterTwo, ...chapterThree].map((s, i) => ({
  id: s.id, discipline_id: 'financial-analysis', order_index: i + 1,
  title: s.title, subtitle: s.subtitle, summary_markdown: `${s.summary}\n\n${expandedGuides[s.id]}`
}));

const letters = ['a', 'b', 'c', 'd', 'e', 'f'];
const allSourceSegments = [...chapterOne, ...chapterTwo, ...chapterThree];
const anchorsFor = (s) => [...s.summary.matchAll(/^###\s+(.+)$/gm)]
  .map((match) => match[1].toLowerCase().replace(/[^a-z0-9]+/g, '-').replace(/(^-|-$)/g, ''));
const questions = allSourceSegments.flatMap((s) => s.facts.map(([question, answer, explanation, anchor], i) => {
  const targetIndex = i % 6;
  const distractors = Array.from({ length: 5 }, (_, j) => s.facts[(i + j + 1) % s.facts.length][1]);
  let di = 0;
  const options = letters.map((letter, pos) => ({
    id: letter,
    text: pos === targetIndex ? answer : distractors[di++]
  }));
  const optionReasoning = options
    .filter((option) => option.id !== letters[targetIndex])
    .map((option) => {
      const sourceFact = s.facts.find((fact) => fact[1] === option.text);
      if (!sourceFact) return `<strong>Option ${option.id.toUpperCase()}:</strong> This does not answer the question as asked.`;
      return `<strong>Option ${option.id.toUpperCase()}:</strong> This is relevant to “${sourceFact[0]}” (${sourceFact[2]}) but does not answer the current question.`;
    });
  const difficulty = i < 4 ? 'easy' : i < 8 ? 'intermediate' : 'hard';
  return {
    id: `q_${s.id}_${i + 1}`, segment_id: s.id, order_index: i + 1, difficulty,
    question, options, correct_option: letters[targetIndex],
    explanation: `${explanation}\n\n### Check the alternatives\n${optionReasoning.join('\n\n')}`,
    pool_type: 'quiz', section_reference: s.title, section_anchor: anchorsFor(s)[Math.floor(i / 4)] || anchorsFor(s)[0] || anchor
  };
}));

// Keep correct answers from becoming a length cue. For any lesson with more than two
// longest-correct items, enrich only the extra distractor with its own fact explanation.
for (const s of allSourceSegments) {
  const lessonQuestions = questions.filter((q) => q.segment_id === s.id);
  const longestCorrect = lessonQuestions.filter((q) => {
    const correctLength = q.options.find((o) => o.id === q.correct_option).text.length;
    return correctLength === Math.max(...q.options.map((o) => o.text.length));
  });
  for (const q of longestCorrect.slice(2)) {
    const correctLength = q.options.find((o) => o.id === q.correct_option).text.length;
    const candidates = q.options
      .filter((o) => o.id !== q.correct_option)
      .map((o) => ({ option: o, fact: s.facts.find((f) => f[1] === o.text) }))
      .filter((entry) => entry.fact)
      .sort((a, b) => b.fact[2].length - a.fact[2].length);
    const candidate = candidates.find((entry) => `${entry.option.text} ${entry.fact[2]}`.length > correctLength);
    if (candidate) candidate.option.text = `${candidate.option.text} ${candidate.fact[2]}`;
  }
}

const flashcards = allSourceSegments.flatMap((s) => s.facts.slice(0, 8).map(([front, back, , anchor], i) => ({
  id: `fc_${s.id}_${i + 1}`, segment_id: s.id, order_index: i + 1,
  front, back, category: s.title, section_reference: s.title, section_anchor: anchorsFor(s)[Math.floor(i / 4)] || anchorsFor(s)[0] || anchor
})));

const workbookExercises = allSourceSegments.map((s) => ({
  id: `wb_${s.id}`, segment_id: s.id, order_index: 1, title: `Applied case: ${s.title}`,
  difficulty: 'hard', prompt_markdown: s.workbook[0], solution_markdown: s.workbook[1],
  section_reference: s.title,
  section_anchor: anchorsFor(s)[0] || s.facts[0][3]
}));

export const financialAnalysisSeed = {
  discipline: {
    id: 'financial-analysis', name: 'Financial Analysis & Management Control', code: 'FAMC-2026',
    description: 'Accounting principles, financial statements, ratio and trend analysis, and business valuation using the supplied NOVA IMS course materials.'
  },
  segments, questions, flashcards, workbookExercises
};
